\import{macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{frap}
\title{How the notes are organized}

\p{The developed mathematics follows one practical question: what data must a
move carry so that we can later compose moves and test reachability?
[Incidence, symmetry and moves](frap-0002) answers it in order. First,
[polytopes, flags and facet symmetry](frap-0008) describe the shape and show
their role in triangle and tetrahedron examples. Next, [facet moves](frap-0009)
turn a local facet rotation into an action on incidence positions, with
tetrahedron and cube calculations that make the construction concrete. Only
then do [positions, orientations and global constraints](frap-000A) separate a
piece's destination from transport in its local frame, while [transfer and the
effect of dimension](frap-000B) connect that transport with a group
homomorphism and compute the relevant local abelianizations.}

\p{The full route returns from general mathematics to solving and play.
The later stages are linked outlooks, with the concrete cube examples
above as their starting point:}

\ol{
  \li{\strong{General Rubik puzzles:} the incidence actions, framed
  orientations and transfer developed below.}
  \li{\strong{The [layered-cube family](frap-0003):} piece types, layer
  turns and conventions as #{N} varies.}
  \li{\strong{The [three-layer cube](frap-0004):} complete state
  coordinates and a constructive characterization of reachability.}
  \li{\strong{[Human methods and computer search](frap-0005):}
  recognizable cases, algebraic effects, usability, move costs and optimality.}
  \li{\strong{[Return to play](frap-0006):} notation, scrambling,
  recognition, replay and complete solves.}
}

\p{[Further connections](frap-0007) link the developed orientation and
transfer calculations with separate outlooks on
[label transport and topology](frap-0014) and
[scrambling and search functions](frap-0015).
These branches can be read alongside the main route.}
